/**
 * @mainpage OpenTLV C++ API reference
 *
 * Start with namespace tlv, the class index, or the public header index.
 * Include tlv++/tlv.hpp for the complete header-only wrapper API.
 * C declarations are linked to the accompanying C reference and are excluded
 * from the C++ indexes. Using the wrappers still requires linking the C library.
 *
 * @section compatibility Language compatibility
 * This reference shows the C++11 fallback interfaces of tlv::any, tlv::span,
 * tlv::expected and tlv::unexpected, together with the C++20 tlv::TlvCodec
 * concept. In newer language modes, compat.hpp selects standard-library
 * aliases: byte and any from C++17, span from C++20, and expected/unexpected
 * when the standard library advertises C++23 expected support.
 * The fallback interfaces are minimal; they do not promise every operation
 * of the standard-library equivalents. See compat.hpp for their contracts.
 *
 * @section ownership Ownership and lifetime
 * tlv::reader and tlv::writer hold their C state by value and borrow buffers
 * and format contexts. Destroying a wrapper does not release that storage.
 * tlv::bytes and tlv::entry are non-owning views; keep the source bytes alive.
 * tlv::expected destroys its held value or error through RAII, and tlv::any
 * owns its type-erased value. Error messages, the registry and convenience
 * encoding can allocate; the C core's allocation-free guarantee does not
 * apply to every C++ helper. Individual declarations specify these contracts.
 */

/**
 * @namespace tlv
 * @brief Public C++ wrappers, value types, codec traits and helpers for OpenTLV.
 *
 * Wrappers translate C result codes into tlv::expected results with tlv::error.
 * Refer to each declaration for borrowed lifetimes and error behavior.
 */
